# Venture Brief

CASE FRAMING DOCUMENT
LGIT – Medical Device Expansion (CoF-Based Technologies)
Case Typology
This case is primarily classified as:
•	2A — Market Entry (Leveraging Existing Capabilities into a New Industry Vertical)
With a secondary evaluation component of:
•	1A — External Investment / Partnership Assessment (Startup collaboration or acquisition as entry enabler)
0. Information Gaps (Pre-Framing)
1.	Does the $350M revenue target apply to a single segment or combined segments?
2.	If combined, is simultaneous multi-segment participation permitted, or must each segment independently justify entry?
3.	What capital allocation range is acceptable for this initiative?
4.	What level of implantable-device liability exposure is acceptable?
5.	Is ISO 13485 recertification planned regardless of this decision, or conditional upon it?
6.	Will this initiative operate as a dedicated business unit or within existing divisions
7.	Are there predefined internal return hurdles beyond the stated IRR target?
8.	Are there organizational constraints (e.g., headcount limits, manufacturing site restrictions) that must be treated as fixed boundaries?
9.	Is progression from feasibility study to PoC considered automatic upon technical validation, or subject to separate commercial approval criteria?

1. Context
LGIT possesses advanced PCB and CoF (Chip-on-Film) substrate manufacturing capabilities, including fine-pattern photolithography and plating technologies capable of approximately 10µm-level circuit formation on rigid and flexible substrates.
These capabilities enable:
•	Ultra-thin, flexible circuit formation
•	High-density, fine-pattern electrode structures
•	Multi-channel implementation on compact films
•	Enhanced device miniaturization and design flexibility
LGIT’s primary businesses today focus on packaging, optical, and mobility solution technologies serving the IT and automotive markets.
The company is exploring entry into high-value medical device markets, specifically:
•	Continuous Glucose Monitoring Systems (CGMS)
•	Nerve stimulation devices
•	Brain–Computer Interface (BCI) systems
The strategic rationale historically discussed includes:
•	Structural shifts toward personalized, preventive, AI-enabled healthcare
•	Growth in wearable monitoring demand
•	Increasing prevalence of neurological and cardiovascular diseases
•	Rising need for thin, flexible, high-density electrode substrates in implantable and wearable devices
LGIT is not currently operating as a certified medical device manufacturer. However, it has established partial regulatory and technical groundwork:
•	ISO 13485:2016 certification held from December 2018 to December 2021
•	Completion of four ISO 10993 biocompatibility evaluation items:
o	Cytotoxicity
o	Implantation
o	Intracutaneous reactivity
o	Systemic toxicity
Beyond fine-pattern fabrication, LGIT possesses additional capabilities relevant to medical electrode and sensor production:
•	Biocompatible films and materials including LCP, PET films, Au, Ag/AgCl, and Carbon
•	Roll-to-Roll (R2R) mass-production infrastructure
•	Low-temperature chip bonding technology designed to minimize surface damage on PET films with low heat resistance
•	De-noising technology under development to correct signal distortion caused by noise and unwanted biosignals
LGIT is conducting a joint research project with KAIST in the BCI domain. This collaboration includes:
•	Co-development of electrode substrates
•	Development of communication modules
•	Development of operating modules
•	Animal toxicity evaluation of developed operating modules
•	Planning for EEG data acquisition and utilization
LGIT does not own proprietary CGM sensor technology and does not have a development history of biosignal monitoring sensors for consumer wearable devices. However:
•	The company is currently developing CGM sensor substrates and modules in collaboration with a major CGM company
•	It is in discussion with a leading neurostimulation company regarding joint development of neurostimulation electrodes
•	It has more than five years of technical development experience in neurostimulation electrode technology through collaboration with a startup
All three counterparties across CGM, neurostimulation, and BCI applications have signed NDAs with LGIT.
Prior to entering formal PoC contracts, LGIT is currently conducting feasibility study projects with three separate companies to review and confirm its technical capabilities in each respective application area.
These feasibility studies are positioned as structured pre-PoC validation steps. Upon successful completion, the expected next phase would be formal PoC agreements. These activities represent technical validation efforts and do not yet constitute commercial-scale engagement.
The company’s strategic focus is not on finished end devices. It is evaluating participation at the level of:
•	Core components and modules for small wearable sensors
•	Key components and modules for implantable devices such as neurostimulation and BCI systems
The company is not pursuing an OEM finished-device business model. Instead, it is considering:
•	Component supply
•	Module-level participation
•	Potential CDMO-type participation
The strategic direction under evaluation includes expansion from substrate manufacturing into higher-value participation within the medical device value chain.
M&A is permitted as part of the entry strategy. The company is considering the acquisition of early-stage startups with system-solution capabilities, such as Echo. While no specific investment amount has been set, LGIT recently made an investment of approximately USD 35 million, providing a reference scale for potential transactions.
Echo partnership is currently in the exploration phase. LGIT has entered into an NDA with Echo and is exploring collaboration at the development stage. The intention is to assess Echo alongside other potential candidates to identify the most suitable strategic partner.
Regulatory preparation is recognized as a central requirement for entry into the medical device sector. This includes:
•	Medical-grade material certifications (e.g., ISO 10993)
•	Manufacturing facility certifications (e.g., ISO 13485)
•	Identification of additional regulatory requirements not yet fully mapped
•	Assessment of business implications associated with compliance
The financial modeling horizon for this initiative considers:
•	The projected market position three years from entry
•	The subsequent three years of expansion
•	A total six-year modeling period

Why Now
The timing of this evaluation is influenced by:
•	Industry-wide movement toward personalized, preventive, and AI-enabled healthcare
•	Expansion in wearable monitoring and biosensing applications
•	Growth in neurological and cardiovascular disease prevalence
•	Increasing demand for thin, flexible, high-density electrode substrates
•	Existing internal technical groundwork and collaborative projects
•	Active NDAs, feasibility studies, and commercial discussions with global device companies
These elements collectively create a strategic inflection point for evaluating whether scaling participation is appropriate.
2. Decision
Should LGIT commit to scaled commercial participation in the medical device industry?

3. Key Questions
BLOCKING (Must Be Resolved to Answer the Decision)
1.	Are CGM, neurostimulation, or BCI markets individually sufficient to support $350M annual revenue by Year 3 post-entry?
2.	Can LGIT realistically achieve an operating margin above 30%, IRR above 20%, and payback within three years in at least one target segment?
3.	Does LGIT possess, or can it realistically build within an acceptable timeframe, the regulatory, manufacturing, and commercial capabilities required for scalable participation?
4.	Do ongoing feasibility studies, NDAs, and commercial contracting discussions represent credible progression toward scalable commercial demand, or are they limited to technical validation without durable revenue conversion potential?
5.	Do structural barriers (regulatory lock-in, OEM vertical integration, IP moats, switching costs) materially constrain the feasibility of scaling?
6.	Are the expected margin ranges across participation levels (10–15% standard substrate, up to 40% customized substrate, 30–60% modules/components) economically defensible under realistic assumptions?

HIGH PRIORITY (Material to the Decision)
7.	Where do structural profit pools concentrate across the medical device value chain (standard substrate, customized substrate, module, CDMO/system participation)?
8.	Is substrate-only participation economically sufficient to meet stated financial targets, or would vertical expansion be required?
9.	Should CGM, neurostimulation, and BCI be prioritized, sequenced, or pursued in parallel based on economic return, regulatory burden, and risk exposure?
10.	What execution model (internal capability build, partnership, acquisition, hybrid) is feasible and economically rational given LGIT’s capability gaps and risk tolerance?
11.	What characteristics must a startup partner possess to materially increase the probability that LGIT achieves its financial and strategic targets?
12.	How attractive are potential startup partnerships, if any, relative to standalone internal development?
13.	How are startups positioned across materials, components, modules, systems, and services in the CGM, neurostimulation, and BCI ecosystems?
14.	What do investment and M&A activities by MedTech leaders and major technology companies signal regarding defensible value pools and likely consolidation dynamics?
15.	How do leading competitors structure participation (component vs module vs CDMO), and what differentiates them across materials, technology, manufacturing, quality, regulatory capability, sales, and service models?
MEDIUM PRIORITY (Contextual but Non-Blocking)
16.	Can ISO-certified production lines be shared with non-medical applications without compromising compliance integrity or creating regulatory risk?
17.	What GTM model is required for each participation level and segment?
18.	What milestone-based roadmap would be required to scale within the six-year horizon?
4. Success Criteria
Financial Outcomes
•	$350M annual revenue by Year 3 post-entry
•	IRR greater than 20%
•	Operating margin above 30%
•	Payback period within three years
Strategic Outcomes
•	Establishment of a defensible position in a high-growth medical device segment
•	Structural revenue diversification beyond commodity substrate markets
•	Participation in value pools with sustainable differentiation
•	Capability expansion beyond substrate fabrication where justified
•	Creation of optionality for expansion into adjacent medical verticals
Operational Outcomes
•	Regulatory burden proportionate to opportunity size
•	Controlled exposure to implantable-device liability risk
•	Scalable manufacturing feasibility consistent with R2R infrastructure
•	Organizational capacity to support medical-grade quality systems
Explicit trade-offs that must be clarified:
•	Component-only vs Module-level participation
•	Internal development vs Buy & Borrow strategy
•	Wearable (CGM) vs Implantable (Neuro/BCI) exposure

5. Assumptions
1. Market Growth
[Inferred — requires market validation]
At least one of the target segments (CGM, neurostimulation, or BCI) will sustain sufficient market size and growth over the six-year modeling horizon to support $350M annual revenue by Year 3 post-entry under realistic market share assumptions.

2. Substrate Criticality
[Inferred — requires technical validation]
Ultra-thin, fine-pattern flexible substrates are a technically critical enabling component in at least one target segment and cannot be substituted without material performance, reliability, or regulatory impact.

3. OEM External Sourcing
[Inferred — requires market validation]
OEMs in at least one target segment will continue to externally source a meaningful portion of substrate or module requirements over the modeling horizon, preserving a viable addressable market for LGIT.

4. Value Pool Accessibility
[Inferred — requires competitive validation]
Higher-value participation tiers (customized substrate, module, CDMO/system participation) are commercially accessible to qualified new entrants and allow margins materially above standard substrate supply.

5. Margin Achievability
[Client-stated financial ranges]
Margin ranges of 10–15% (standard substrate), up to 40% (customized substrate), and 30–60% (modules/components) are achievable under realistic cost, pricing, and scale conditions.

6. Regulatory Achievability
[Inferred — requires regulatory assessment]
ISO 13485 recertification and expanded ISO 10993 compliance can be achieved within a timeframe and cost envelope consistent with the six-year financial model.

7. Production Line Flexibility
[Inferred — requires operational validation]
ISO-certified production lines can be configured to serve medical applications without materially impairing broader manufacturing efficiency or creating unacceptable compliance risk.

8. Partnership Risk Reduction
[Inferred — requires comparative validation]
Partnership-led or acquisition-led entry meaningfully reduces time-to-market or execution risk relative to standalone internal development.

9. Financial Target Consistency
[Client-stated targets]
Achievable unit economics and adoption rates are consistent with meeting $350M revenue by Year 3, >20% IRR, >30% operating margin, and three-year payback.

10. Margin Sustainability
[Inferred — requires competitive validation]
Competitive intensity in target segments will not compress margins below levels required to achieve stated financial targets before LGIT reaches minimum viable scale.

6. Constraints
Decision Deadline
Leadership decision required by April 15, 2026.
Geographic Scope
Initial focus on North America and Europe, with potential expansion thereafter.
Time Horizon for Projections
Six years total, including three years to initial target state and three subsequent expansion years.
Fixed Boundaries
•	Existing PCB and CoF technological capabilities are already built.
•	The company is not pursuing a finished-device OEM strategy.
•	Medical regulatory compliance requirements cannot be bypassed.
•	Current ISO certifications are expired and must be re-established for certified medical manufacturing.

