Technology Background

The company possesses the technological capabilities required for PCB and CoF substrate manufacturing processes, and its substrate technology expertise includes plating and photolithography technologies that enable fine patterning on both rigid and flexible substrates. These technologies are intended to be applied to nerve stimulation devices, BCI components, and CGMS patches.

In particular, the company’s CoF technology enables the formation of fine circuits on both sides of thin film-type substrates. Based on micro-circuit formation technology at the 10 µm level, the company can implement functions on smaller films as circuit line spacing becomes narrower.

Since circuits can be formed on ultra-thin films, this technology can be applied to flexible products, enhancing the freedom of device design and usability.

Purpose

Applying precision-patterned and flexible electrode technologies to achieve accurate neural stimulation or brain-signal interpretation, enabling signal acquisition from diverse locations in the brain.

Enhancing signal accuracy and enabling miniaturization for glucose monitoring through electrochemical electrode technology and precision chip-bonding processes.

If the strategy and hypotheses prove valid, the objective is to analyze the company’s existing capabilities to identify areas that can be leveraged and those that can be expanded, and to propose the direction for capabilities that need to be supplemented (from a Build / Buy / Borrow perspective).

The company aims to enter high value–added markets in the medical device sector based on its substrate technologies, focusing on nerve stimulation and BCI technologies that enable continuous monitoring, as well as wearable-type CGMS for glucose monitoring. The company seeks to advance and validate these initiatives through a strategy that expands its capabilities from substrates to modules and system-level solutions.

① Trend / Market
Verifying the Validity of Approaching the CGMS and BCI Markets with CoF Substrate Technology
Background

The company intends to enter the medical device market by leveraging its CoF substrate technology and is reviewing potential application areas such as:

Thin-film wearable CGMS (continuous glucose monitoring systems)

Nerve stimulation

BCI

The company believes that the wearable CGMS market continues to expand due to growing demand for daily health monitoring, and that the need for nerve stimulation and BCI technologies is also increasing as the number of patients with cardiovascular and neurological disorders continues to rise.

From a technical perspective:

CGMS devices are attached to the skin in patch form

Thin substrates and fine-patterning technologies enable accurate measurements while minimizing user discomfort

For nerve stimulation and BCI technologies:

Devices must precisely read and transmit brain signals

Ultra-thin substrates and fine-pattern technologies are essential to avoid irritation or discomfort on the brain surface

Across CGMS, nerve stimulation, and BCI applications, the following are core technological competencies:

Fine-patterning flexible substrate technologies

High-density electrodes

Multi-channel implementation capabilities

These elements are expected to serve as key differentiators in the market.

Hypothesis

The CGM and neurostimulation markets are expected to experience explosive growth, and LGIT can achieve differentiation by leveraging its substrate technology capabilities.

② Identifying Market Inflection Points, Customer Pain Points, and Value Delivery
Background

Based on the historical evolution of the medical device industry, the market has transitioned from a diagnosis-and-treatment-centric paradigm to a preventive, AI-enabled healthcare model.

The key trigger driving market transformation is believed to be the shift toward personalized, preventive medical devices.

As the market moves toward personalized devices, the company’s substrate technology enables the development of thin, fine-patterned structures capable of delivering accurate information without discomfort or a sense of foreignness.

This capability addresses customer pain points, particularly:

Need for enhanced portability

Reduced inconvenience in device use

By alleviating these pain points, the technology can deliver:

A more comfortable user experience

Reduced psychological resistance for implantable devices

Greater user reassurance

This is especially relevant for nerve-stimulation and BCI systems.

③ Customer / Competitor
Value Chain Analysis of CGMS, Nerve Stimulation, and BCI Devices
Background

The value chains of CGMS, nerve stimulation, and BCI have not yet been clearly defined.

However, it is anticipated that key components such as:

Glucose-monitoring sensors

Neuro-stimulation electrodes

are operated directly by final module suppliers such as:

Medtronic

Abbott

This structure appears to stem from the need for:

Stringent safety assurance

Optimized profit-structure management

Therefore, a comprehensive analysis is needed of:

End-to-end value chains

Profit pools

across CGMS and nerve stimulation / BCI systems.

This analysis will help determine:

Strategic positioning choices made by major players

Which business model configuration is most appropriate for the company

It is also necessary to examine why some companies operate across multiple value-chain stages.

Example:
Jabil, a global manufacturing solutions provider, expanded into medical devices by leveraging capabilities in:

Design

Manufacturing

System assembly

Understanding which competencies enabled Jabil’s entry into medical devices and where profit pools exist will provide insights into the market structure.

Hypothesis

In the medical device supply chain, profit will come not only from device manufacturers but also from suppliers of critical components and materials such as LGIT.

④ Customer / Competitor
Identifying Potential Competitors with Similar Substrate Technologies
Background

The company has not yet fully entered the medical field.

However, companies with stretchable-substrate or FPCB-based technologies may also have the potential to enter the medical device components market.

By examining such companies, the company can better assess:

Future competitive landscape

Possible market-entry strategies

Example:

Linxes (France)
Has shifted its business toward medical strips and patch-type flexible substrates over the past 3–4 years and is reportedly generating revenue from this line of business.

This transition cannot be explained solely by its legacy strengths in:

Smart cards

Connectors

Analyzing Linxes’ strengths and success factors may offer strategic insights.

Another example:

Murata (Japan)
Murata is expanding into the medical sector using stretchable-based technologies.

Given Murata’s strong competencies in materials and substrates, it may scale into adjacent medical fields.

Therefore, it is necessary to closely investigate signals of Murata’s expansion into medical devices.

Hypothesis

Based on cases from similar substrate manufacturers entering the medical device components market, the most significant potential competitors are expected to be:

Murata

FPCB manufacturers

⑤ Portfolio Expansion
Strategic Assessment of Business Competitiveness and Differentiation
Background

Visibility into the value chains of BCI and CGMS technologies remains limited.

Competitive dynamics and structural attributes of CoF-based or adjacent technological domains are not sufficiently transparent.

The objective is to analyze how key players have positioned themselves when entering the medical device sector:

Pure-play component suppliers

Integrated businesses spanning upstream and downstream

This analysis will inform the company’s future business architecture and portfolio configuration.

Since the organization has no prior footprint in the medical device domain, it is necessary to evaluate the importance of obtaining critical certifications such as:

ISO 10993 (biocompatibility / material certification)

Component-level conformity assessments

ISO 13485 (medical manufacturing quality systems)

Another key question is whether a pre-certification engagement model is viable, or whether there are risk-mitigated pathways for phased market entry.

Hypothesis

High value-added opportunities can be captured at the substrate level alone, and possessing substrate and component technologies may enable smooth expansion into module- and system-level businesses.

⑥ Portfolio Expansion
Investigation of Success and Failure Cases of Manufacturing Companies Expanding into Medical Devices

Examples include CDMO players such as:

Integer

Flex

Jabil

Background

Analyzing cases where component or manufacturing companies expanded into the medical device market provides insights for the company’s own expansion strategy.

Companies with:

Metal machining

Precision manufacturing capabilities

have often entered categories such as:

Implants

Surgical tools

These areas align well with existing manufacturing competencies and have relatively lower clinical responsibility.

Failure cases often involve products that directly contact the human body, where companies were unable to meet strict:

Technical requirements

Safety standards

Deficiencies in medical-grade performance and safety compliance acted as major barriers.

Hypothesis

Component manufacturers are evolving beyond contract manufacturing and parts supply toward the CDMO model.

With strengthened quality management and cost competitiveness, success in the medical device domain is increasingly achievable.

⑦ Venture Capital
Review of Startup Activity in Neurostimulation / BCI / CGM
Background

The organization is evaluating entry into the CoF-based BCI sector through a partnership with Echo.

It is therefore necessary to map the technological trajectories and business architectures of startups in:

CGMS

Neurostimulation

BCI ecosystems

Startups should be categorized by their position in the value stack:

Materials

Components

Modules

Systems

Services

This helps identify:

Partnership opportunities

Complementarity with internal capabilities

Market observations show that North America is seeing strong investment in brain-control and brain-sensing innovation, with early-stage and growth-stage startups attracting significant capital.

In addition to traditional MedTech leaders such as:

Medtronic

Abbott

Big Tech companies including:

Apple

