## Additional Context

1. Context
LGIT possesses advanced PCB and CoF (Chip-on-Film) substrate manufacturing capabilities, including fine-pattern photolithography and plating technologies capable of approximately 10µm-level circuit formation on rigid and flexible substrates.
These capabilities enable:
•	Ultra-thin, flexible circuit formation
•	High-density, fine-pattern electrode structures
•	Multi-channel implementation on compact films
•	Enhanced device miniaturization and design flexibility
LGIT’s primary businesses today focus on packaging, optical, and mobility solution technologies serving the IT and automotive markets.
The company is exploring entry into high-value medical device markets, specifically:
•	Continuous Glucose Monitoring Systems (CGMS)
•	Nerve stimulation devices
•	Brain–Computer Interface (BCI) systems
The strategic rationale historically discussed includes:
•	Structural shifts toward personalized, preventive, AI-enabled healthcare
•	Growth in wearable monitoring demand
•	Increasing prevalence of neurological and cardiovascular diseases
•	Rising need for thin, flexible, high-density electrode substrates in implantable and wearable devices
LGIT actually has prior ISO certifications, wider technical capabilities beyond substrate-related technologies, and ongoing academic collaboration projects. 
LGIT has a capable team that knows how to procure and achieve both 13485 and 10993 certifications, which is strongly corraborated by certification history and current motion.:
History:
•	ISO 13485 : 2016 – Certified in December 2018; expired December 2021 (Certification No. MD696831). No reason to believe that LG Innotek will not be able to successfully receive a new one.

Current progress:
•	ISO 10993 – Completed 4 out of 10 evaluation items:
• 10993-5: Cytotoxicity
• 10993-6: Implantation
• 10993-10: Intracutaneous reactivity
• 10993-11: Systemic toxicity

Core Technologies & Production Infrastructure Beyond 10 μm Fine Patterning
•	Biocompatible films/materials: LCP, PET films; Au, Ag/AgCl, Carbon materials
•	Mass-production infrastructure: Roll-to-Roll (R2R) manufacturing line
•	Low-temperature chip bonding technology: Minimizes surface damage on PET films with low Tg/heat resistance
•	De-noising technology (in development): Corrects signal distortion caused by noise and unwanted biosignals

LGIT is conducting a joint research project with KAIST in the BCI domain. This collaboration includes:
•	Co-development of electrode substrates
•	Development of communication modules
•	Development of operating modules
•	Animal toxicity evaluation of developed operating modules
•	Planning for EEG data acquisition and utilization
LGIT does not own proprietary CGM sensor technology and does not have a development history of biosignal monitoring sensors for consumer wearable devices. However:
•	The company is currently developing CGM sensor substrates and modules in collaboration with a major CGM company
•	It is in discussion with a leading neurostimulation company regarding joint development of neurostimulation electrodes
•	It has more than five years of technical development experience in neurostimulation electrode technology through collaboration with a startup
LGIT is pursuing entry into the medical electrode market using its CoF-based Tape Substrate Package Solution.
In parallel, LGIT possesses strengths in:
•	Optics Solutions: camera modules, 3D sensing modules, actuators
•	Mobility Solutions: autonomous driving sensing, wireless connectivity, lighting
These capabilities allow LGIT to position itself as a partner with substrate/module/ regulatory/reliability competencies for medical device customers. 
The company is not pursuing an OEM finished-device business model. Instead, it is considering:
•	Component supply
•	Module-level participation
•	Potential CDMO-type participation

M&A is permitted as part of the entry strategy. 
The company is considering the acquisition of early-stage startups with system-solution capabilities, such as Echo. No specific investment amount has been set, benchmarking of recent acquisitions indicates that potential investment budgets would be between $20-40M.
Echo partnership is currently in the exploration phase. LGIT has entered into an NDA with Echo and is exploring collaboration at the development stage. The intention is to assess Echo alongside other potential candidates to identify the most suitable strategic partner.
LGIT has signed NDAs with multiple global top-tier companies in the CGM and BCI fields.
LGIT is currently discussing three different applications, Neuro / BCI / CGMS with three separate companies. All three companies have already signed NDAs with LGIT. Before moving to an official PoC contract, they are now working on feasibility study projects to review and confirm LGIT’s technical capabilities. After the feasibility studies are completed, they expect to move forward with formal PoC projects

The financial modeling horizon for this initiative considers a 6 year period:
•	The projected market position 3 years from entry
•	The subsequent three years of expansion
•	 Thus a total 6 year modeling period

Why Now
The timing of this evaluation is influenced by:
•	Industry-wide movement toward personalized, preventive, and AI-enabled healthcare
•	Expansion in wearable monitoring and biosensing applications
•	Growth in neurological and cardiovascular disease prevalence
•	Increasing demand for thin, flexible, high-density electrode substrates
•	Existing internal technical groundwork and collaborative projects
•	Active NDAs and commercial discussions with global device companies
These elements collectively create a strategic inflection point for evaluating whether scaling participation is appropriate.

Team and talent

The task initiative leader is SungKyu (SK) Bae who is an experienced executive at LG Innotek with a strong technical background in automotive and electromechanical engineering. He is currently serving as a leader in the company's research and business development sectors, with a specific focus on Medical Device Task initiatives

LG Innotek employs professionals with Medical Regulatory and Quality Affairs expertise to support its expanding healthcare and medical component business. While the company is primarily known as a global electronic component manufacturer, its strategic shift into Digital Healthcare and Medical Devices. There is absolutely no talent or skill gap required to execute appropriate certification and compliance procedures. Especially given that LG Innotek has done it before and is doing it now.

LG Innotek also has sufficient leadership to execute all the needed motions for receiving needed ISO certifications and other Medical, Regulatory and Quality Affairs tasks.

Assumptions:
1. Market Growth
